.TH JOIN 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
join \- relational join of two sorted files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B join
.RB [ \-a
.IR file_number ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR string ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR char ]
.RB [ \-1
.IR field ]
.RB [ \-2
.IR field ]
.I file1
.I file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B join
reads
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
which must already be sorted in byte-collating order on their
respective join fields, and for each pair of lines whose join fields
compare equal, writes one output line combining them. Exactly one of
.I file1
and
.I file2
may be
.B \-
to mean standard input; both may not be
.BR \- .
.PP
.B join
never sorts or verifies the sort order of its input.
.PP
Unless
.B \-t
is given, each line is split into fields on runs of one or more
<blank> characters, with leading <blank> characters ignored entirely
and never producing an empty leading field. This is a plain
whitespace tokenization, and is not the same field-splitting rule
.BR sort (1)
applies by default.
.PP
Without
.BR \-o ,
each output line consists of the join field, followed by the
remaining fields of the matching
.I file1
line, followed by the remaining fields of the matching
.I file2
line, each separated by the output field separator. For an unpairable
line printed because of
.BR \-a ,
there is no counterpart to draw fields from, so the output is simply
that line's own fields, with the join field first; no placeholder
fields are added for the missing side.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a " file_number"
In addition to the normal matched output, also print each unpairable
line of file
.I file_number
(1 or 2). May be given for both files, as \fB\-a 1 \-a 2\fR.
.TP
.BI \-e " string"
Within
.BR \-o 's
output only, replace any empty output field with
.IR string .
This has no effect on the default output format used when
.B \-o
is not given.
.TP
.BI \-o " list"
Construct each output line from exactly the fields named in
.IR list ,
instead of the default layout described above.
.I list
is one or more field specifiers, separated by <comma> or <blank>
characters:
.B 0
for the join field, or \fIfile_number\fR.\fIfield\fR (\fIfile_number\fR
1 or 2,
.I field
a positive field number) for a specific field of one input line.
.B \-o
may be given more than once; each occurrence appends to the same
output field list. A field named in
.I list
that does not exist on a given output line (including every
.I file2
field of an unpairable
.I file1
line printed under
.BR \-a ,
or vice versa) is an empty output field, subject to
.BR \-e .
.TP
.BI \-t " char"
Use
.I char
as the field separator: every occurrence of
.I char
on a line is significant, so adjacent occurrences produce an empty
field between them, and leading occurrences produce an empty first
field.
.I char
also becomes the output field separator, replacing the default single
<space>.
.TP
.BI \-1 " field"
Join on field
.I field
of
.I file1
(default 1).
.TP
.BI \-2 " field"
Join on field
.I field
of
.I file2
(default 1).
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file1
The pathname of the first input file, or
.B \-
for standard input.
.TP
.I file2
The pathname of the second input file, or
.B \-
for standard input.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.B \-v
(print only unpairable lines, suppressing the normal matched output)
is not implemented, and is refused with a diagnostic rather than
silently mapped onto
.BR \-a .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The files were joined and written successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: an invalid option or option argument, a missing,
extra, or doubled
.B \-
operand, an unreadable input file, or a write failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sort (1),
.BR comm (1),
.BR uniq (1)
